* ci(upstream-sync): an upstream sync reaches main only after a full suite on the fork's runners [daveey]

The 10-29 sync (1a221b37e16 -> 39ac60111ad) was pushed straight to main; main's busy-branch
concurrency cancelled both push runs, so upstream's new tests first ran on the fork's runners an
hour later and failed there (tmux). The updater's fork_merge_upstream path pushed main the same way.

- scripts/ci/upstream_sync.py: the gate. Stages a candidate on sync/upstream-<sha>; lands only a
  head whose upstream-sync.yml run completed green with both Python slices and e2e succeeding on
  self-hosted runners at exactly that sha (ff, else a non-forced merge commit). Refuses red,
  pending or unreadable evidence; one candidate in CI at a time; 6 h backoff after a red run;
  lands other hosts' green candidates; twins of a landed sync are not restaged.
- .github/workflows/upstream-sync.yml: full suite per candidate, own run_id concurrency group
  (never cancelled by main).
- hermes update (fork_merge_upstream): the off-tree merge is handed to the gate; the live tree
  no longer moves to an untested merge. The strictly-behind push goes through the gate too.
- scripts/check --commit (the pre-push hook): refuses any branch push that brings upstream
  commits the fork's main lacks, unless the gate pushes it. Live in installed hooks without
  reinstall (the hook runs the pushed tree's scripts/check).

Tests: real bare remotes and real pytest verdicts (red refused, green lands), the updater path
through the real gate subprocess, and the pre-push hook refusing a hand-pushed sync.
Card t_01a18bb880ab1f28.

