* fix(desktop): a remote-primary window no longer cold-starts a local hermes serve for hub replica and retained-Kanban reads

Core E2E remote-topology failed "the client spawned no backend of its own"
(main dispatch 40667086106): Electron spawned a forced-local
conn:local::default serve in a window whose gateway is a remote.

Two renderer reads pinned the registry 'local' (This device) entry from the
primaryGatewayConnectionId() 'local' fallback, which main force-spawns under
a global remote:
- hubGet() of the replica connection (hub-sync sidebar, ad2b99b23): now passive.
- retained-Kanban getSession() for rows without connection_id: now pinned only
  to a real primary connection id (primaryGatewayConnectionPin).

Bisect: first bad ad2b99b23, last good 662710b38. New tests red on base, green
here; e2e/core remote-topology + remote-secondary pass.

* fix(desktop): pin retained-Kanban reads without growing gateway.ts

primaryGatewayConnectionId('pin') returns the real primary id or null, so a
remote-primary window does not pass the 'local' fallback that makes Electron
cold-start a local hermes serve. gateway.ts stays at its main line cap.

* fix(desktop): keep primaryGatewayConnectionId() a string; pin via pinnedPrimaryGatewayConnectionId()

Main's primary-recents caller passes primaryGatewayConnectionId() as a string, so
widening it to null | string broke the desktop typecheck on the merge. The pin
read gets its own null-returning getter; gateway.ts stays at its main line cap.

* fix(cron): agent jobs are held, not failed, while the gateway restarts for an update [daveey]

hermes-hub cron 6b4342ad83d9 recorded three FAILED runs (10-29/30), each seconds after a
`hermes update` restarted the gateway: the Desktop serve ticker took over the store inside the
restart gap, dispatched the agent job, and connect_gateway answered "Gateway draining:
update_paused", which run_canonical_job booked as a failed run (failure_streak 3).

Root: "gateway not ready" was a held fire on the headless tick path only and a job failure on
every other tick path.

- scheduler_tick: the gateway gate now runs for every tick with no gateway owner bound in the
  process (headless crontab ticks and the Desktop/standalone tickers), decided by the owner
  binding rather than the entry point, so a new ticker path is gated by default. The gateway's
  own ticker (owner bound) skips the probe.
- scheduler_gateway_gate: a live update fence holds agent jobs (ensure would answer
  update_paused even over a running gateway); an interactive ticker (may_start) may still let
  ensure start an absent gateway outside an update; headless never spawns.
- scheduler_authority: a GatewayUnavailableError before admission in draining/starting/absent
  raises CronFireNotAdmitted (the probe-vs-connect race). Refusals after submit stay failures.
- scheduler/jobs: a not-admitted scheduled fire returns its slot to next_run_at, drops the fire
  claim, undoes a one-shot's dispatch bump, stamps last_fire_error and leaves last_status /
  failure_streak alone; the ledger row records "Not admitted". Manual fires still fail loudly.

Tests: tests/cron/test_tick_gateway_not_ready.py (desktop tick during update / fenced ready /
starting / draining holds; race past the gate keeps the job due and streak 0; definite fault and
post-admission refusal still fail; guard: hermes cron tick, InProcessCronScheduler without owner
and _start_desktop_cron_ticker all go through the gate, gateway-hosted skips it).

* kanban: a park's wake_check keeps one live probe across worker nodes; sweep lost probes [daveey]

t_01a18c12ba6895b8 (created on the MacBook, run by the hub as a Board worker node) parked on
'wait-check task t_01a18c31ab3e58f7 --gateway macbook'. The probe row (task_wake_checks,
machine-local, never synced) was written on the hub; worker_node.evict dropped the scheduled copy
on the next tick and run_due_wake_checks' stale sweep deleted the orphaned row. The MacBook got the
scheduled status and event but no probe, so the card slept 43 min past its producer (7 such cards).

- The probe runs on the gateway that parked the card (probe_host on the park event): only it is
  sure to run it (a capability-owner request id lives in that host's request store). evict keeps a
  scheduled copy whose probe is live; the wake is a status write the Board carries home. Clock wakes
  of a copy are left to the home board.
- A capability relay to the home names the home as probe_host: the worker registers nothing and the
  home registers the probe from the park event.
- restore_missing_checks (every dispatcher tick): a scheduled card whose last park carries a
  wake_check and has no probe here is restored once (wake_check_restored); a second loss wakes it
  with a comment (wake_check_lost). A relayed park whose probe host has not said wake_check_alive
  (every 3 h) for 3 h + 2x check_every + 15 min is woken with a comment.

Card t_01a18c702a4e8024. Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_wake_check_home.py (RED on 7a2ccb0b4a),
test_kanban_capability_relay.py.

* fix(gateway): a claimed admission runs after a drain flip; wake drain test joins the drain [daveey] (#268)

The authority-route wake test started the successor while the owner's drain
still had the wake's claim in flight; the successor's epoch bump made the
owner's settlement raise stale_epoch, and the row never reached completed
(flaky on CPU-starved CI runners). A real restart joins in-flight work first
(_drain_active_agents); the test now does too.

The new claim_in_flight case holds the owner's claim inside its write txn
until draining is set, and exposed a lost wake: execute_admission rebuilt the
event without _admitted_before_drain, so run_inbound_hooks' drain gate refused
a row the claim's in-txn gate had already admitted and settled it completed
unrun. Claimed rows are in-flight work, so the event is marked admitted.

* test(browser): wait for stand-in browsers to finish exec before scanning their argv [daveey] (#280)

Popen returns once the child has begun execve, but /proc/<pid>/cmdline can still be
empty: 112 of 1000 immediate psutil cmdline reads came back [] on the hub. _pid_naming_dir
correctly fails closed on an empty argv, so a starved runner that scanned before the
stand-in finished exec got None (CI run 41026597300). The stand-in now touches a ready
marker once running and the test waits for it (120s cap, returns as soon as it exists);
the child lives until stdin EOF instead of a fixed sleep(60) lease.

* fix(update): an unanswered idle probe while waiting on a review is not the review ending

A CPU-starved backend can miss the 3s HTTP timeout; the wait then stopped it unfenced
mid-review window (no commit before SIGTERM). Treat no answer as transient while the pid is
alive (deadline still bounds), and raise the probe timeout to 10s.

* fix: use psutil for pid liveness

